# Line-ending policy. Everything checks out as LF on every platform, so a
# Windows clone cannot rewrite text files to CRLF. This repository depends on
# exact bytes more than most: a certificate is bound to the hash of the
# original problem file, the tamper corpus and hand-worked verifier vectors are
# compared verbatim, and the determinism gate compares two runs of the same
# trace with `cmp`. A CRLF rewrite breaks those comparisons for a reason that
# is slow to diagnose.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Instance and certificate fixtures are hashed as-is. Never normalize them.
# The JSON schema is an instance format here as well as a data format, so a
# file under it is hashed like any other model; the trace is bound to the
# certificate of its run by length and checksum, so its bytes are exact too.
*.mps -text
*.lp -text
*.hcc -text
*.hct -text
*.canon -text

# Every hand-worked vector, at every depth, for the same reason and one more:
# a carriage return is a different instance here rather than a stray byte to
# tidy up, so the readers refuse one instead of normalizing it (ADR-0006). A
# fixture that git had quietly stripped a carriage return out of on the way
# into the index would be a fixture testing the opposite of what it says.
#
# The tree rather than a list of extensions, because a pattern carrying a
# slash does not reach into a subdirectory: `spec/v1/vectors/json/*.json`
# covered the vectors beside it and left every rejection fixture under
# `reject/` on the default policy. The frozen `.canon` dumps are compared with
# `assert_eq!` against `include_str!`, and they were on it too.
spec/v1/vectors/** -text

# Binary — never convert
*.gif binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
